The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
scx_task_iter_init(&sti, ...);
for_each_scx_task(&sti, p) {
    scx_ops_enable_task(p, ...);  // changes calling thread's sched_class from fair to ext
}
```
Reason: During the READY → ENABLED task switching loop in `scx_enable()`, the calling thread’s own sched_class is switched from fair to ext. Since the fair class has higher priority than ext, a saturating fair-class workload can indefinitely starve the calling thread, causing a system hang.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
helper = kthread_run_worker(0, "scx_enable_helper");
sched_set_fifo(helper->task);   // RT (SCHED_FIFO) priority, cannot be starved by fair/ext
kthread_init_work(&cmd.work, scx_enable_workfn);
kthread_queue_work(READ_ONCE(helper), &cmd.work);
kthread_flush_work(&cmd.work);
```
And the enable logic is moved to `scx_enable_workfn` running on this RT kthread.
Reason: By offloading the entire enable body to a dedicated RT (SCHED_FIFO) kthread, the priority inversion is avoided—the RT kthread has higher priority than any fair or ext class task, so it cannot be starved. The calling thread simply queues and waits, while the actual sched_class switching occurs in a starvation‑safe context.

